  Paste this into the target project's CLAUDE.md to activate team voting for that project.
  Fill in TEAM_SPACE_NAME with the "spaces/AAAAxxxxxxx" ID from the list_chat_spaces tool.
-->

## Team decisions via Google Chat

When you would normally call `AskUserQuestion` for a decision that affects the whole team
(not a quick clarification only I could answer, e.g. architecture choices, which approach to
ship, naming/API decisions) — post it to the team instead:

1. Call `post_team_vote` with `spaceName: "TEAM_SPACE_NAME"`, the question, and 2-9 options.
   Each option gets a tappable reaction for a quick vote; the message also tells everyone they
   can reply "/<number>" (e.g. "/2", or "/2 because it's simpler") to put an answer on record.
   Anything else in the thread is just discussion. Only the first valid "/<number>" reply from
   anyone counts as the decision.
2. Do NOT poll `sync_team_vote` yourself turn-by-turn (that burns a full reasoning turn, and
   tokens, per check). Instead, run a single backgrounded shell script that loops internally —
   sleep 5 seconds, call the MCP tool via its underlying request, repeat — for up to 2 hours,
   and only stops when `answer` is non-null. You are only re-engaged once, when that background
   job finishes; you spend no tokens while it's waiting.
3. As soon as `answer` is non-null, that's the final decision — stop and use it.
   `sync_team_vote` already posted the confirmation, cleaned up the poll message, and removed
   its own seed reactions (leaving genuine teammate reactions visible) for you. Then call
   `record_vote_usage` once with your best count/estimate of tokens spent on this question
   (from posting the poll through detecting the answer) and your model name — it gets appended
   to the end of the poll message so the team can see what each decision cost.
4. If nobody answered within the 2 hours, fall back to asking me directly with
   `AskUserQuestion`, using the same question and options, and mention that the team poll went
   unanswered so I know why you're asking me directly instead of waiting longer.

Purely local/implementation-detail questions (things only I would know, like "which file did
you mean") should still go to me directly via `AskUserQuestion` — don't spam the team space
with those.
